{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And Aaron returned to Moses, to the entrance of the tent of meeting, and the plague was stayed.</b> Scripture has already testified that as soon as Aaron came and offered the incense, the plague was immediately stayed — before he returned to the entrance of the tent of meeting. If so, why was this verse necessary? Rather, it teaches you that Aaron bent the angel and led him before Moses to the entrance of the tent of meeting. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: Aaron found the angel standing and destroying; he stood opposite him and would not let him destroy, as it says, ״ויעמד בין המתים ובין החיים״ — 'and he stood between the dead and the living.' Aaron said to him: 'Moses sent me.' The angel said to him: 'You are the emissary of flesh and blood, and I am the emissary of the Holy One, blessed be He.' Aaron said to him: 'Behold, the Holy One, blessed be He, and Moses are at the entrance of the tent of meeting — let us go to them.' The angel paid no heed until Aaron seized him by his waist and led him there, as it says, ״ושב אהרן אל משה פתח אהל מועד והמגפה נעצרה״. R. Yitzchak said: this teaches that he led him to the entrance of the tent of meeting and stayed him there — this is what is meant by 'the entrance of the tent of meeting, and the plague was stayed.' When he came to depart from the world, what is written? (Deuteronomy 33:11) ״ברך ה' חילו ופועל ידיו תרצה״ — 'May HaShem bless his substance, and accept the work of his hands' — this refers to the incense that was in his hand, with which he bent the angel and seized him by his waist, to make known to you how precious the incense is. Thus far in the Tanchuma."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:33:32.438277936Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}